Chapter: ProbeArithEdges
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The arithmetic edges, which a tier file cannot hold because each one kills an
 arm.

 ABS AT THE MINIMUM INTEGER has no answer. The magnitude of -9223372036854775808
 is 9223372036854775808, which is one past what an i64 can hold, so every
 implementation has to choose: wrap, saturate, or refuse.

 Bare metal wraps, silently and by construction. `emit-abs-builtin`
 (Emit/X86_64Builtins.codex:387) is three instructions -- `test`, `jge`, `neg`
 -- and two's-complement `neg` of the minimum is the minimum again. The
 overflow flag is set and nothing reads it. So `abs minInt` answers minInt, a
 NEGATIVE number from a function whose whole contract is to return a
 non-negative one, and no diagnostic anywhere.

 The plug emits `@as(i64, @intCast(@abs(x)))`. `@abs` widens to the unsigned
 type, so the magnitude is representable and it is `@intCast` that cannot take
 it back -- a panic in Debug, undefined in a release mode.

 So this is finding 30's shape a second time: the plug stricter than the
 language it implements, killing a program upstream runs. The prediction is
 that bare metal prints `-9223372036854775808` and carries on to the last line,
 and this arm dies at the abs.

 The rows before it are the safe neighbourhood, so that a death at the edge
 still leaves evidence that everything approaching it agreed.

 __NARROW IS CLEAN, and it is recorded here so nobody re-derives it. It reads
 like a checked narrowing and the plug emits it as a bare pass-through, which
 is the exact shape of finding 28. It is not: `emit-narrow-builtin`
 (Emit/X86_64Builtins.codex:362) is `emit-expr st (list-at args 0)` and nothing
 else. Both arms pass the value through untouched, so its 42 call sites across
 the compiler carry no divergence. The check it is named for lives in the type
 checker, not in the emitted code.

Section: Instrument

  show-yn : Boolean -> Text
  show-yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni "-- the safe neighbourhood --"
   print-line-uni ("abs 5                      : " & integer-to-text (abs 5))
   print-line-uni ("abs -5                     : " & integer-to-text (abs (0 - 5)))
   print-line-uni ("abs of minInt + 1          : " & integer-to-text (abs (0 - 9223372036854775807)))
   print-line-uni ("that one is positive       : " & show-yn (abs (0 - 9223372036854775807) > 0))
   print-line-uni "-- __narrow is a pass-through on both arms --"
   print-line-uni ("narrow keeps the value     : " & show-yn (__narrow 12345 == 12345))
   print-line-uni ("narrow keeps a negative    : " & show-yn (__narrow (0 - 12345) == (0 - 12345)))
   print-line-uni "-- the edge: abs of the minimum integer --"
   print-line-uni ("abs minInt                 : " & integer-to-text (abs (0 - 9223372036854775807 - 1)))
   print-line-uni ("it came back NEGATIVE      : " & show-yn (abs (0 - 9223372036854775807 - 1) < 0))
   print-line-uni "-- reached the end, so this arm wrapped rather than refusing --"
  end
